         [0m[49;1H[0m[K [38;5;248mAlso note: the two gate failures map to the two blocker codes. If canonical_comparison_status carried "fail", then blocker list would be redundant-ish. The design: structural statuses all green (resolved/pass/pass), disposition blocked due to gate failures captured in blocker_codes. That matches "use blocked when any admitted gate blocks" — disposition is separate from the structural statuses.[39m                                                                                                       [0m[51;1H[0m[K [38;5;248mNow source_match: value options "resolved"/"pass"/boolean. Given the other statuses are lowercase words, "pass" fits. Evidence: storage_source_receipt identifies the doc; extraction_receipt parse_errors=0, duplicates=0, missing metadata=0; comparison exact matches all true → the extracted content matches the source. source_match="pass". Hmm, alternatively "resolved" meaning source resolved to a concrete document with hash. Both plausible. The instruction enumerated "resolved/pass/pass" — if    [0m[52;1H[0m[K [38;5;248mintended for the last three status fields (dependency, migration, canonical_comparison), then source_match is unconstrained by that phrase and I choose from evidence. "pass" vs "resolved": For a [39;38;5;248msource[39;38;5;248;38;5;248m — you resolve a source. For a [39;38;5;248mregistry[39;38;5;248;38;5;248m — you resolve targets. Hmm.[39m                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       [0m[53;1H                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     