rase it'd be four values listed. So source_match likely gets "pass" too... or the phrase intentionally covers only three and expects source_match="pass" as obvious.[39m                                                                      [0m[36;1H[0m[K [38;5;248mActually wait. Maybe simpler: the phrase "use resolved/pass/pass when supported" lists values in field order for the status fields excluding source_match? Field order of status fields: source_match, dependency_registry_status, migration_coverage_status, canonical_comparison_status. If we map in order: source_match=resolved, dependency_registry_status=pass, migration_coverage_status=pass, canonical_comparison_status=? Not covered → must decide from evidence: fail? [39m                               [0m[38;1H [38;5;248mHmm, that's Reading C: canonical_comparison_status="fail" because canonical_global_validation_status="fail". But then the field name mismatch: "canonical_comparison_status" vs "canonical_global_validation_status". The receipt distinguishes exact-match comparisons (true) from global validation (fail). The objective says "A failing canonical currentness check ... requires a blocked disposition." Blocked disposition comes from the currentness check failing. If canonical_comparison_status were     [0m[39;1H [38;5;248mmeant to carry the fail, then disposition blocked + blocker stale_generated_index_artifact still holds.[39m                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            [0m[40;1H                                                                                                                                                                       